# PDS Meridian

Meridian now has an installable, strictly typed package foundation, immutable
typed evidence inventory, deterministic consumer-side adapter registry, and
bounded Core catalog discovery and canonical-verification preparation layer,
exact evidence serialization, an immutable digest-bound projection cache, a
read-only publication/evidence diagnostics surface, and three real optional
ScoreForm v0.10.0, Quillan v0.9.0, and Concord v0.2.0 adapters in the
released `0.1.1` baseline. The current unreleased v0.2 development tree
qualifies ScoreForm v0.11.0, Quillan v0.10.0, and Concord v0.3.0, adds the explicit
#27-#35 interpretation layers, formally adopts Core's neutral
`grouping_signal_set_v1` interchange in #36 against exact Core v0.6.3, and
implements the teacher-controlled grouping-signal derivation policy in #37
and deterministic content-addressed Meridian grouping-signal derivation in #38,
then #39 preview/review and #40 immutable Core/CSV export.
The package can prepare an authorized
`AdapterProjectionRequest`, persist an exact validated `EvidenceInventory`, apply
the explicit #27-#35 interpretation layers, calculate/persist deterministic
Grade Item-level and Academic Period standards proficiency, and persist/select
exact immutable `meridian.grouping_signal_policy` revisions, and generate
immutable `meridian.grouping_signal_derivation` snapshots without mutating
historical state, preview/review them deliberately, and export approved signals
through Core with immutable receipts and optional Core-native CSV. It does not
yet calculate Grades, provide #41 task-oriented teacher workflows, generate
reports, create subscriptions, or deliver reports.

## Purpose

Meridian will transform authorized producer-owned academic evidence into
explainable standards proficiency, Grades, and reports under explicit, versioned
policies.

Its eventual responsibilities include:

- publication selection and evidence eligibility;
- Grade-item membership;
- evidence, attempt, reassessment, and exclusion policy;
- standards-proficiency calculation;
- conventional, standards-based, and hybrid Grade policies;
- Academic Period aggregation;
- teacher overrides;
- report composition;
- provenance-bound report snapshots;
- report subscriptions; and
- coordination with authorized delivery components.

Meridian consumes Core-governed registrations and Publication Records while
preserving the meaning and authority of producer-owned records.

## Package status

The current package provides:

- the `pds-meridian` distribution and `meridian` import package;
- one authoritative version declaration and the Core v0.6 dependency boundary;
- the side-effect-free `meridian` help/version command and read-only
  publication/evidence diagnostics;
- strict mypy, Ruff, pytest, package, and cross-platform CI validation;
- immutable `meridian.evidence` models preserving exact native meaning;
- immutable `meridian.adapters` exact keys, descriptors, requests, and registry;
- fail-closed adapter selection and lazy producer-reader readiness checks;
- immutable `meridian.ingestion` discovery, canonical-context, authorization,
  series-observation, and prepared-invocation models;
- bounded typed discovery through Core's disposable Academic Catalog;
- canonical Publication Record, registration, series, and withdrawal reload;
- deterministic catalog-to-canonical drift detection;
- Core-owned producer-profile compatibility evaluation;
- exact adapter selection using canonical Core models only;
- explicit deployment authorization before manifest access;
- Core path and digest verification followed by bounded immutable byte loading;
- an in-memory SHA-256 handoff invariant through `AdapterProjectionRequest`;
- final canonical-state rechecking before a prepared request is returned;
- exact `EvidenceInventory` mapping conversion with closed value variants;
- immutable projection snapshots with canonical JSON, cache keys, and digests;
- authorization-before-cache-read and read-only current-state assessment;
- producer-neutral `meridian.diagnostics` support/readiness models;
- bounded publication listing and exact canonical compatibility verification;
- authorization-gated inspection/explanation of persisted evidence snapshots;
- privacy-safe stable `ingestion.*`, `contracts.*`, `adapters.*`, and `cache.*`
  outcomes;
- exact verification of the official Core v0.6.3 wheel;
- wheel and source-distribution validation and isolated-wheel smoke testing; and
- reusable documentation and repository validation tooling.
- explicit, lazy adapters for exact ScoreForm v0.11.0, Quillan v0.10.0, and
  Concord v0.3.0 academic results.
- immutable standards-evidence association decisions with explicit SHA-bound
  current selection and Core durable-standard resolution; and
- deterministic, bounded Grade Item/student/standard/scale aggregation inputs
  preserving mapped observations, native states, and exact exclusion causes;
- pure deterministic Grade Item-level standards-proficiency calculation with
  explicit `highest`, `lowest`, `median`, and `mode` policy strategies; and
- immutable SHA-bound proficiency policy/result histories, explicit current
  selection, deterministic explanation, and pure staleness diagnostics.
- pure producer-neutral Academic Period standards-proficiency aggregation over
  exact immutable #34 results with explicit `direct`/`descendants` scope; and
- immutable SHA-bound Academic Period proficiency policy/result histories,
  explicit current selection, deterministic explanation, and pure freshness
  diagnostics;
- formal adoption of Core's `grouping_signal_set_v1` as the sole shared
  planning-signal interchange, preserving `pds-core>=0.6.3,<0.7`; and
- focused plus isolated installed-wheel qualification of Core's canonical signal
  model/JSON, `grouping_signal_csv_v1`, immutable exchange storage, canonical
  signal digest, and workspace-aware roster diagnostics with Concord absent; and
- immutable teacher-controlled grouping-signal derivation policy revisions over
  exact #35 Academic Period proficiency, exact Core period/standard identity,
  exact proficiency-scale positions, explicit contextual band partitions,
  independent missing/insufficient handling, and CAS-protected selection;
- deterministic `meridian.grouping_signal_generation` over the exact Core roster,
  exact selected/current #35 state, and rebuilt current #35 inputs, producing
  rich immutable content-addressed Meridian derivations with structured
  freshness/mismatch blockers and no Core signal write.

The package does **not** yet provide:

- Portia or Vitrine adapters;
- duplicated producer manifest decoding or semantic validation;
- institutional identity or authorization policy;
- report snapshots or issued-report persistence;
- Grade calculations or task-oriented #41 teacher planning-export workflows;
- report generation, persistence, subscriptions, or delivery; or
- production-ready functionality.

Package installation alone does not enable publication ingestion.

## Typed evidence inventory

`meridian.evidence` defines the immutable destination for later consumer-side
producer adapters. Each item preserves exact Core publication context, projection
identity, a privacy-minimal student subject, producer-native target and result
kind, a closed typed native value, ordered native provenance, and explicit
eligibility status.

The inventory preserves points, exact native scales, explicit non-score states,
and superficially similar values without assuming equivalence. It does not select
attempts, map native scales, evaluate eligibility policy, calculate proficiency,
calculate Grades, or select attempts.

See
[`docs/architecture/typed-evidence-inventory.md`](docs/architecture/typed-evidence-inventory.md)
for the complete model boundary and synthetic examples.

## Standards evidence and aggregation inputs

`meridian.standards_evidence` separates producer-declared alignment from an
explicit teacher/policy association and from a later calculation. Its pure
builder consumes only a caller-supplied bounded candidate set, preserves the
exact Grade Item revision, student, durable Core standard, target scale, and
upstream decision/profile provenance, and performs no arithmetic.

`meridian.standards_evidence_storage` persists immutable association revisions
beneath exact work membership, hashes the full logical association identity for
Windows-safe paths, and uses an explicit SHA-bound compare-and-swap
`current.json`. See [standards-evidence association and aggregation inputs](docs/architecture/standards-evidence-association-and-aggregation-inputs.md).

## Grade Item standards proficiency

`meridian.standards_proficiency` now consumes one exact #33 aggregation input
snapshot plus one exact calculation-policy revision and target proficiency
scale. It calculates `highest`, `lowest`, `median`, or `mode` strictly over
configured scale positions, preserves native/noncontributing states, and emits
structured `insufficient_evidence` rather than converting absence to zero.

`meridian.standards_proficiency_storage` persists immutable policy and result
revisions with SHA-256 sidecars and explicit compare-and-swap `current.json`
selection. Persisted results embed the exact #33 input body and bind exact
policy, scale, algorithm, and calculation-fingerprint provenance. Freshness is
a pure comparison that reports `inputs_changed`, `policy_changed`,
`scale_changed`, and/or `algorithm_changed` without recalculation or mutation.

See [Grade Item standards-proficiency calculation](docs/architecture/standards-proficiency-calculation.md).

## Academic Period standards proficiency

`meridian.academic_period_proficiency` consumes exact immutable #34 Grade Item
proficiency results and exact #28 membership provenance to build one bounded
class/Academic Period/student/standard calculation. `direct` scope requires the
complete Grade Item membership basis to match the exact target period;
`descendants` permits that complete basis to fall within the exact target Core
calendar subtree. No date, label, lifecycle, or current-period inference is
used.

A mixed or otherwise incompatible exact membership basis is retained as
`period_scope_mismatch` and is always blocking in schema version 1. A missing
#34 result is `missing_result`; an existing #34 `insufficient_evidence` result
remains insufficient; and a calculated low proficiency remains a real ordinal
result. Policy independently chooses whether missing and insufficient entries
are noncontributing or blocking.

`meridian.academic_period_proficiency_storage` persists immutable policy and
result revisions with exact dependency validation, SHA-256 sidecars, and
explicit compare-and-swap current selectors. Freshness is diagnostic only: a
stale result remains immutable and is never automatically recalculated or
selected.

See [Academic Period standards-proficiency aggregation](docs/architecture/academic-period-proficiency-aggregation.md).

## Core neutral grouping-signal interchange

Issue #36 adopts Core's released `grouping_signal_set_v1` as Meridian's only
shared planning-signal interchange contract. The contract first shipped in Core
v0.6.1; Meridian's active and authenticated qualification baseline is Core
v0.6.3, so the runtime floor remains:

```text
pds-core>=0.6.3,<0.7
```

Core remains authoritative for the typed signal model, canonical JSON,
`grouping_signal_csv_v1`, immutable exchange storage, canonical signal-byte
SHA-256, and workspace-aware roster diagnostics. Meridian adds no competing
wire model, serializer, CSV contract, storage layer, or roster matcher.

A future Meridian-generated signal will be a minimal explicit projection from
richer Meridian-owned academic derivation state:

```text
Meridian private academic derivation
    -> Core grouping_signal_set_v1
    -> optional downstream planning consumer
```

Bands are contextual ordinal planning signals rather than Grades, proficiency
labels, ability classifications, or permanent learner attributes. Signal
identity is exact and immutable as `(class_id, signal_set_id)`, there is no
automatic `current` or `latest` selection, partial roster coverage is valid,
student identity is exact `student_id`, and `source.snapshot_digest` is distinct
from Core's canonical signal-record digest.

The #36 installed-wheel qualification deliberately runs with exact Core plus
Meridian and proves the grouping-signal contract without Concord installed.
Issue #37 adds the separate teacher-controlled derivation-policy layer.
Issue #38 now generates the immutable rich Meridian derivation; preview/acceptance
and Core/CSV export remain issues #39 and #40.

See [Core neutral grouping-signal interchange](docs/architecture/core-grouping-signal-interchange.md).

## Teacher-controlled grouping-signal derivation policy

`meridian.grouping_signal_policy` defines immutable v1 policy over one exact
#35 Academic Period proficiency basis: exact period/calendar revision, durable
standard, exact #35 policy, exact proficiency scale, one explicit contextual
`dimension_id`, teacher-defined contiguous scale-position bands, fixed
`same_level_same_band` tie handling, and independent `noncontributing`/`blocking`
handling for missing and insufficient results.

`meridian.grouping_signal_policy_storage` persists SHA-256-bound contiguous
policy revisions beneath class-local Meridian state. Creating a revision never
selects it; `current.json` is an explicit digest-bound selector updated only by
compare-and-swap. Storage validates the exact Core class/Academic Period/standard
and exact #35 policy/scale dependencies on write and selection. #37 stores no
student assignments and writes no Core signal or CSV.

See [Teacher-controlled grouping-signal derivation policy](docs/architecture/grouping-signal-derivation-policy.md).

## Deterministic grouping-signal generation

`meridian.grouping_signal_derivation` defines the immutable rich #38 snapshot,
privacy-minimal exact Core roster basis, per-student source/disposition
provenance, canonical JSON, calculation fingerprint, and content-addressed
`gsd_<fingerprint>` identity. `meridian.grouping_signal_derivation_storage`
persists those snapshots as immutable JSON/SHA-256 pairs with no revision family
and no `current` or `latest` pointer.

`meridian.grouping_signal_generation_basis` rebuilds the exact current #35 input
basis from explicit current Grade Item, membership, and #34 result selections.
`meridian.grouping_signal_generation` then resolves the selected #37 policy,
exact Core roster, explicitly selected #35 results, and existing #35 freshness
semantics before producing/persisting a derivation or deterministic structured
blockers. It writes no Core grouping signal, exports no CSV, and does not invoke
Concord.

See [Deterministic grouping-signal generation](docs/architecture/grouping-signal-generation.md).

## Immutable Core/CSV grouping-signal export

Issue #40 resolves the explicitly selected `accepted_for_export` review, rechecks
live #38 currentness, delegates canonical signal storage and CSV grammar to Core,
persists a privacy-minimal Meridian export receipt, and never launches Concord or
creates Groups. See [Immutable Core/CSV grouping-signal export](docs/architecture/grouping-signal-core-export.md).

## Adapter interface and registry

`meridian.adapters` defines the consumer-side adapter protocol and an immutable,
explicit registry. Selection derives one exact key from validated Core
Publication Record and Academic Work Registration models and matches producer,
publication kind, manifest contract, producer contract, and source-record
contract without nearest-version, newest-version, producer-only, capability-only,
or generic fallback.

Reader availability is checked lazily through distribution metadata only during
explicit invocation. The selected adapter must return an `EvidenceInventory`
whose Core provenance and `ProjectionIdentity` agree exactly with the request and
descriptor. Optional ScoreForm v0.11.0, Quillan v0.10.0, and Concord v0.3.0 adapters are
available through explicit composition; there is no adapter entry-point group or
unconditional producer runtime dependency.

Install the authenticated ScoreForm release wheel first, then install Meridian's
exact optional dependency with `python -m pip install ".[scoreform]"`. Installing
ScoreForm does not register the adapter. See
[`docs/architecture/scoreform-adapter.md`](docs/architecture/scoreform-adapter.md).

Likewise, install the authenticated Quillan release wheel and then use
`python -m pip install ".[quillan]"`. Quillan does not auto-register its adapter.
See [`docs/architecture/quillan-adapter.md`](docs/architecture/quillan-adapter.md).

Install the authenticated Concord v0.3.0 release wheel and then use
`python -m pip install ".[concord]"`. Concord does not auto-register its adapter,
and Meridian does not read Concord Artifact contents through this adapter. See
[`docs/architecture/concord-adapter.md`](docs/architecture/concord-adapter.md).

See
[`docs/architecture/adapter-interface-and-registry.md`](docs/architecture/adapter-interface-and-registry.md)
for the complete selection, invocation, error, and side-effect boundary.

## Catalog discovery and canonical verification

`meridian.ingestion` implements the preparation stages before producer parsing.
Every discovery request wraps a finite Core `PublicationCatalogQuery`. Returned
`CatalogPublication` values remain candidate observations only; Meridian reloads
the exact canonical Publication Record, referenced and current registration
state, complete publication series, and withdrawal state before continuing.

Catalog observations are compared with canonical authority. Any difference fails
closed as deterministic candidate drift. Meridian then delegates producer
compatibility to Core, selects an exact adapter using canonical models, checks
reader distribution readiness without importing the producer, and requires an
explicit deployment authorization decision before manifest access.

After authorization, Core verifies the canonical manifest path and SHA-256.
Meridian reads bounded immutable bytes, constructs `AdapterProjectionRequest`,
and repeats canonical reload so a withdrawal, supersession, registration change,
or disappearance during verification cannot produce a mixed observation. The
result is `PreparedPublicationInvocation`; the adapter is not invoked.

See
[`docs/architecture/catalog-discovery-and-canonical-verification.md`](docs/architecture/catalog-discovery-and-canonical-verification.md)
for the complete catalog, canonical-state, drift, compatibility, authorization,
manifest, race, error, and privacy boundary.

## Requirements

Current unreleased Meridian development requires:

```text
Python >=3.11
pds-core>=0.6.3,<0.7
```

Current development and CI qualification use the exact completed Core v0.6.3,
ScoreForm v0.11.0, Quillan v0.10.0, and Concord v0.3.0 releases. The released
Meridian v0.1.1 history remains Core v0.6.0 and Quillan v0.9.0; the active #33
floor/qualification update does not rewrite that release fact. Core is
distributed through GitHub Release artifacts rather than PyPI.

## Development installation

Install the official Core wheel, then install Meridian in editable development
mode:

```powershell
python -m pip install .\pds_core-0.6.3-py3-none-any.whl
python -m pip install -e ".[dev,scoreform,quillan,concord]"
python -m pip check
meridian --version
meridian --help
```

The current official Core qualification artifact is:

```text
pds_core-0.6.3-py3-none-any.whl
SHA-256: 98d7596ce0eed26e4d56a17bbbbd644db3014259b56a45783a173fe8237af5e5
```

Do not substitute a sibling Core checkout or locally rebuilt wheel when running
the baseline artifact-authentication and installed-wheel checks.

## Command line

Meridian now exposes read-only publication and persisted-evidence diagnostics:

```text
meridian
meridian --help
meridian --version

meridian publications list --workspace <workspace> [filters]
meridian publications verify <publication_id> --workspace <workspace>

meridian evidence inspect <publication_id> <cache_key> --workspace <workspace> --purpose-id <purpose>
meridian evidence explain <publication_id> <cache_key> --workspace <workspace> --purpose-id <purpose>
```

`publications list` uses a finite Core catalog query and reloads canonical state
before reporting it. `publications verify` starts from exact canonical state and
does not require the disposable catalog. Metadata diagnostics report producer
compatibility, Meridian adapter support, and reader distribution/version
readiness without opening producer manifests or invoking adapter projection.

Persisted evidence commands are security-sensitive. They operate only on one
exact immutable projection snapshot and require a deployment-provided
`PublicationAuthorizer` through the existing `read_projection_cache` boundary.
The stock console application intentionally provides no production authorizer,
so evidence commands fail closed unless an embedding deployment injects one.
There is no command-line authorization bypass.

All new data commands support deterministic text and `--format json` output.
These diagnostics do not select evidence, calculate proficiency, calculate
Grades, or generate reports.

See
[`docs/architecture/evidence-inventory-and-diagnostics.md`](docs/architecture/evidence-inventory-and-diagnostics.md).

## Local validation

From an activated repository virtual environment, run:

```powershell
.\run_tests.ps1 `
  -CoreWheel C:\path\to\pds_core-0.6.3-py3-none-any.whl `
  -ScoreFormWheel C:\path\to\scoreform-0.11.0-py3-none-any.whl `
  -QuillanWheel C:\path\to\quillan-0.10.0-py3-none-any.whl `
  -ConcordWheel C:\path\to\pds_concord-0.2.0-py3-none-any.whl
```

The cross-platform validator is:

```text
python scripts/validate_repository.py --core-wheel <core-wheel> --scoreform-wheel <scoreform-wheel> --quillan-wheel <quillan-wheel> --concord-wheel <concord-wheel>
```

Use `--allow-dirty` during development. The default complete validation requires
a clean repository.

The validator runs:

- Core, ScoreForm, Quillan, and Concord release-wheel authentication;
- installed Core and producer-reader verification;
- `pip check`;
- pytest;
- Ruff;
- strict mypy;
- documentation validation;
- wheel and source-distribution builds;
- Twine checks;
- wheel-content validation;
- isolated installed-wheel smoke testing;
- `git diff --check`; and
- working-tree cleanliness validation.

See
[`docs/development/package-foundation.md`](docs/development/package-foundation.md)
for details.

## Core v0.6 integration baseline

Core v0.6 owns shared canonical infrastructure, including:

- the neutral immutable `grouping_signal_set_v1` contract, canonical JSON/CSV
  conversion, exchange storage, signal-byte digest binding, and roster
  diagnostics;
- immutable Academic Period Calendar revisions and explicit current pointers;
- immutable Academic Work Registration revisions and explicit current pointers;
- immutable Publication Records and separate Publication Withdrawals;
- publication-series identity, supersession, and withdrawal relationships;
- exact workspace-relative producer-manifest paths and SHA-256 binding;
- publication compatibility metadata;
- producer-facing registry services;
- canonical retrieval and validation; and
- the disposable SQLite academic catalog.

Core does not calculate standards proficiency, determine Grade-item membership,
select attempts, calculate Grades, or compose final cross-module reports.

The package foundation and evidence inventory use Core's public contract constants
and immutable models only. They do not read or write a Core workspace.

The active ingestion architecture is documented in
[`docs/architecture/core-v0.6-publication-ingestion.md`](docs/architecture/core-v0.6-publication-ingestion.md).
The internal projection destination is documented in
[`docs/architecture/typed-evidence-inventory.md`](docs/architecture/typed-evidence-inventory.md).

## Canonical ingestion sequence

The implemented preparation layer follows this sequence:

```text
query the disposable Core catalog for bounded candidates
    -> reload the canonical Publication Record by exact publication_id
    -> reload the exact referenced registration revision when academic
    -> reload current registration state separately when academic
    -> reconstruct the canonical publication series and withdrawals
    -> fail closed on catalog-to-canonical drift
    -> evaluate the exact Core producer profile
    -> select an exact Meridian consumer adapter from canonical models
    -> check producer-reader distribution readiness without importing it
    -> enforce deployment authorization for the requested source and purpose
    -> verify the canonical manifest path and SHA-256 through Core
    -> read bounded immutable bytes
    -> construct AdapterProjectionRequest
    -> reload canonical state and reject a changed observation
    -> return PreparedPublicationInvocation
```

Later producer-adapter work parses through the producer-owned public reader,
projects native values into `EvidenceInventory`, and applies Meridian-owned
eligibility, grading, or reporting policy only afterward.

`registry/catalog.sqlite` remains rebuildable, disposable, and nonauthoritative.
Catalog rows do not authorize access or replace canonical verification.

## Routing profiles, producer profiles, and Meridian adapters

Paper Data Suite exposes independent Core discovery groups:

```text
paper_data_suite.modules
paper_data_suite.publication_producers
```

A routing `ModuleProfile` supports PDS2 dispatch. A
`PublicationProducerProfile` contains compatibility metadata. Meridian is a
consumer and declares neither group.

A Core publication profile intentionally contains no parser or callback.
Meridian owns the separate consumer-side adapter layer:

```text
pds-meridian -> pds-core

Meridian producer adapter
    -> producer-owned public manifest contract or reader

producer package
    -> pds-core for registration and publication
    -X-> pds-meridian

pds-core
    -X-> producer-specific parser
    -X-> Meridian grading policy
```

The base package requires Core only. It does not require ScoreForm, Quillan,
Concord, Portia, Vitrine, or another producer package. The registry is composed
explicitly from trusted adapters and performs no entry-point discovery. Real
producer-reader dependencies remain deferred to their adapter issues.

See
[ADR 0003: Adopt Consumer-Side Producer Adapters for Core Publications](docs/decisions/0003-consumer-side-producer-adapters.md).

## Authorization boundary

Discovery is not authorization.

The existence of a catalog row, canonical Publication Record, compatible
producer profile, exact Meridian adapter, installed reader distribution, or
readable filesystem path does not authorize student-record access.

`meridian.ingestion` requires an explicit deployment-provided
`PublicationAuthorizer` before Core manifest verification or byte reading. A
denied decision performs no manifest access. The package defines the typed
request, decision, and enforcement point but does not implement institutional
identity, role, legal, audience, or disclosure policy.

## Producer modules

Producer modules own their native records, public contracts, validation, revision
policy, and domain-specific interpretation. Producer packages must not depend on
Meridian.

ScoreForm, Quillan, Concord, Portia, and future producers have different native
semantics. Meridian must not flatten their outputs into one universal numeric
score.

Unknown producers, known producers with unsupported contracts, and supported
producers with incompatible publications remain distinct future diagnostic
cases.

## Evidence preservation obligations

Later adapters must preserve producer-native meaning, including exact source
identity, attempts, observations, scales, dispositions, non-score states, and
provenance.

Meridian must not automatically convert missing, blank, ambiguous, incomplete,
returned-without-review, excused, excluded, withdrawn, unavailable, or invalid
states to zero.

Question alignment is not a standards rating. A producer rating scale is not a
Meridian proficiency scale unless an explicit, versioned mapping policy says so.

## Grading architecture

Standards-based grading is a first-class Meridian capability. It is not merely a
presentation layer over a conventional points ledger.

A producer publication does not automatically become a Grade item. Grade-item
membership, standards-evidence eligibility, attempt selection, Academic Period
membership, category, weight, and Grade contribution require explicit
Meridian-owned policy.

Meridian must preserve distinctions among producer-native results, work
performance, standards evidence, calculated standards proficiency, Grade-item
results, Academic Period Grades, cumulative course Grades, and report
presentation.

See
[ADR 0001: Policy-Driven Standards Proficiency and Grade Calculation](docs/decisions/0001-policy-driven-standards-proficiency-and-grade-calculation.md)
and its
[Core v0.6 ingestion amendment](docs/decisions/amendments/0001-core-v0.6-ingestion-reconciliation.md).

## Reports and provenance

Meridian reports are derived products. They do not replace or mutate Core
registrations, Publication Records, withdrawals, producer manifests, or
producer-native records.

Issued reports must be provenance-bound snapshots rather than untraceable live
views. A refreshable view must repeat canonical verification against current
authorized state.

See
[ADR 0002: Provenance-Bound Report Snapshots and Subscriptions](docs/decisions/0002-provenance-bound-report-snapshots-and-subscriptions.md)
and its
[Core v0.6 ingestion amendment](docs/decisions/amendments/0002-core-v0.6-ingestion-reconciliation.md).

## Security and synthetic data

Meridian is expected to process sensitive educational information. Public tests,
examples, fixtures, issue reproduction material, and CI artifacts must use only
synthetic data.

Do not commit real student records, rosters, Grades, scans, submissions,
manifests, reports, credentials, private URLs, or workstation paths.

See:

- [`Security.md`](Security.md)
- [Synthetic data policy](docs/development/synthetic-data.md)

